    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Right after Fedya finishes extracting the 'venom' to earn his hundred thousand rubles, Chef Barinov walks in and casually informs everyone that a Far Eastern rat snake is not venomous at all. Listen to the punchline.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Что, мне это снять надо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Да, давай быстрее, начинаем!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Всё, теперь уже готово!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ты только зубами не бойся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тебе сейчас не больно, нет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Да нет, нормально, всё хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вообще-то полоз — не ядовитая змея.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Наш полоз не ядовитый вообще.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ну, конечно, он не ядовитый.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Полоз ведь совсем не ядовитый.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Значит, пятьдесят тысяч за полоза.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ладно, это вполне справедливо.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Что, мне это снять надо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, do I need to take these off?' Dmitry Nagiyev raises the pitch on 'need' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question. Fedya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Да, давай быстрее, начинаем!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes, come on faster, let's start!' Fedya brings out the contrast between 'yes' and 'come on' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Всё, теперь уже готово!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'There, now it is all done!' Fedya brings out the contrast between 'everything' and 'now' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ты только зубами не бойся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Just do not be afraid of my teeth.' Fedya addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'fear'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Тебе сейчас не больно, нет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Does it hurt right now, or not?' Fedya brings out the contrast between 'to you' and 'now' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Да нет, нормально, всё хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, it is fine, everything is good.' The meaning hinges on 'yes' working together with 'well' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вообще-то полоз — не ядовитая змея.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Actually, a rat snake is a non-venomous snake.' In Russian, the idea of 'actually' flows naturally into 'snake' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Наш полоз не ядовитый вообще.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Our rat snake is not venomous at all.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'our' and finishing on 'at all'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Ну, конечно, он не ядовитый.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, of course it is not venomous.' Notice how Russian expresses 'come on' and 'venomous' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Полоз ведь совсем не ядовитый.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'After all, a rat snake is not venomous at all.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'rat snake' before landing on 'venomous'. Dmitry Nagiyev answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nagiyev",
      "t": "Значит, пятьдесят тысяч за полоза.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then fifty thousand for a rat snake.' Dmitry Nagiyev brings out the contrast between 'so' and 'fifty' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Ладно, это вполне справедливо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fine, that is quite fair.' Fedya anchors the line on 'all right', while 'fairly' completes the thought. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
